// str -- the string operations `id` does not have.
//
// There is no substring, no search and no comparison beyond == / !=. What there
// is instead is the flat store, and using it is not an optimisation but a
// **requirement of every builder in this module**: `s = s + chr(c)` in a loop is
// the single most expensive thing a program in this language can do, because
// concatenation allocates and never frees. Building one 1920-character line per
// frame for 1000 frames costs 667 ms and 1.8 GB of retained memory; the same
// work through poke8 plus a single str_of_mem costs 1 ms and 5.8 MB. Both
// numbers are measured, in idem, on this machine.
//
// **Two rules about reading a string, both measured.** The runtime remembers the
// length of the *last* string charat was asked about, and that one-entry memo
// decides the cost of everything here:
//
//   * `len(s)` is a strlen every time it is evaluated, and it is NOT memoised. So
//     `while (i < len(s))` re-measures the whole string per character --
//     quadratic over a 130 KB source. Ask `charat(s, i) >= 0` instead: it is the
//     same end-of-string test, it is O(1), and it is what every per-character
//     loop here does. Where `len` does appear it is once, outside a loop, or
//     over a *list* (where it is a field read, not a strlen).
//   * Alternating charat between two strings misses the memo on every call, so
//     each one pays a full strlen. 200 000 str_eqat calls against a 131 KB source
//     cost 167 ms for that reason alone, against 0 ms for the same number of
//     single-string reads. So str_eqat is right for a test done once per line and
//     wrong inside a per-token loop; str_findat and str_cmp carry the same
//     warning, because they cannot avoid it.
//
// Lifted from idem/engine/core/util/str.id.

// The substring s[i .. i+n). One allocation, no concatenation. A negative or
// over-long n is the caller's problem: n is used as a length, and str_blit
// copies whatever charat answers, which is -1 (a 0xff byte) past the end.
str_slice(string idstd_s, int idstd_i, int idstd_n) {
  word idstd_ad = alloc(idstd_n + 1);
  str_blit(idstd_s, idstd_i, idstd_n, idstd_ad);
  string idstd_ret_s = str_of_mem(idstd_ad, idstd_n);
} return string idstd_ret_s;
("hello world", 0, 5):("hello")
("hello world", 6, 5):("world")
("hello", 2, 0):("")

// n bytes of s from index i, into the store at ad. The one primitive every
// builder in this module is written on top of.
str_blit(string idstd_s, int idstd_i, int idstd_n, word idstd_ad) {
  int idstd_j = 0;
  while (idstd_j < idstd_n) {
    int idstd_charat_v = charat(idstd_s, idstd_i + idstd_j);
    poke8(idstd_ad + idstd_j, idstd_charat_v);
    idstd_j = idstd_j + 1;
  }
} return void;
given idstd_str_t_setup ("hello world", 6, 5, (import str_ad)):() then idstd_str_t_read:("world...........")
given idstd_str_t_setup ("hello world", 6, 0, (import str_ad)):() then idstd_str_t_read:("................")
